00972747802734375, -0.01629638671875, -0.01529693603515625, -0.017486572265625, 0.00537109375, -0.0219879150390625, -0.00030040740966796875, 0.04486083984375, 0.0296173095703125, 0.0028667449951171875, 0.01491546630859375, -0.005329132080078125, 0.00423431396484375, 0.022064208984375, -0.025482177734375, -0.025543212890625, 0.0270538330078125, 0.010406494140625, 0.01056671142578125, -0.0095062255859375, 0.004364013671875, -0.0155181884765625, -0.0005512237548828125, 0.0283966064453125, -0.0095977783203125, 0.0020580291748046875, 0.0089874267578125, -0.00601959228515625, 0.017059326171875, -0.0101776123046875, 0.0035228729248046875, -0.0013484954833984375, 0.019134521484375, 0.02093505859375, 0.0204315185546875, -0.012664794921875, 0.0208892822265625, 0.0225982666015625, 0.00435638427734375, 0.037689208984375, -0.0173187255859375, 0.0186920166015625, 0.0201416015625, -0.04296875, 0.01093292236328125, -0.02203369140625, 0.0166168212890625, 0.0206298828125, -0.03363037109375, 0.00592041015625, 0.00931549072265625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are essential for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is increasingly challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and complex meshed (cyclic) topologies. In such cyclic networks with multiple suppliers and storage units, operational optimization is complicated by nonlinear hydraulic and thermal interactions, inter-temporal coupling via storage, and the combinatorial complexity of flow directions in cycles. Existing methods typically address only acyclic or linearized models, or cannot efficiently guarantee global optimality for realistic, multi-period, cyclic networks with nonlinearities and storage. For example, in a network with two suppliers and a storage tank connected in a loop, determining the optimal flow and temperature profiles over time requires resolving nonlinear pressure drops (which relate flow to pressure loss in pipes), mixing of water at network junctions (which determines outgoing temperatures), and storage dynamics (which couple decisions across time), all while avoiding non-physical cyclic flows\u2014flows that circulate endlessly in a loop without serving any demand. Standard linear or tree-based approaches do not address these challenges. To our knowledge, no prior work provides a global optimization framework for nonlinear, cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, along with scalable algorithmic enhancements and comprehensive evaluation on realistic benchmarks. This work fills that gap.\n\n---\n\n**Main Contributions:**  \n- **Global Optimization Framework:** We develop a global optimization framework for cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, capturing nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints.\n- **Explicit Problem Formulation:** We provide a summary of the full nonlinear mixed-integer optimization problem, including variables, objective, and main constraints, with intuitive explanations of their physical meaning.\n- **Algorithmic Enhancements:** We propose five novel algorithmic enhancements to accelerate spatial branch-and-bound for these networks, each with explicit mathematical or algorithmic formulation and illustrative examples. Full pseudocode and technical details are provided in the supplementary material.\n- **Comprehensive Evaluation:** We provide a detailed evaluation on both generated and real-world benchmark instances, with explicit parameter ranges, statistical analysis, and confidence intervals for key metrics. A summary table of results is included.\n- **Time Decomposition Analysis:** We analyze a time decomposition approach for multi-period operation with storage, rigorously discussing its assumptions, empirical validity, and limitations.\n- **Discussion and Future Work:** We discuss limitations in detail, provide concrete next steps, and outline plans for open-sourcing code and data, including current availability.\n\n---\n\n**Problem Introduction and Mathematical Formulation:**  \nWe consider a district heating network represented as a directed graph \\( G = (V, E) \\), where nodes \\( V \\) correspond to junctions, suppliers, consumers, or storage units, and edges \\( E \\) represent pipes. The main variables and their physical meanings are:\n- \\( q_{e,t} \\): Flow rate (mass per unit time) in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\). Positive or negative values indicate direction.\n- \\( T_{v,t} \\): Water temperature at node \\( v \\) at time \\( t \\).\n- \\( s_{j,t} \\): State of charge (energy stored) for storage unit \\( j \\) at time \\( t \\).\n- \\( x_{e,t} \\): Binary variable indicating flow direction in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) (1 if forward, 0 if reverse).\n\n**Objective:**  \nMinimize total operational cost over all time periods, including supplier fuel costs and storage operation costs:\n\\[\n\\min \\sum_{t} \\sum_{i \\in \\text{suppliers}} c_i(q_{i,t}, T_{i,t}) + \\sum_{j \\in \\text{storage}} c_j(s_{j,t})\n\\]\nwhere \\( c_i \\) and \\( c_j \\) are cost functions for suppliers and storage, respectively.\n\n**Main Constraints (with physical interpretation):**  \n- **Nonlinear pressure drops:**  \n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{e,t} = k_e \\cdot q_{e,t}^2 \\quad \\forall e, t\n  \\]\n  (Pressure loss in each pipe is proportional to the square of the flow, modeling pipe friction.)\n- **Temperature mixing at nodes:**  \n  \\[\n  T_{v,t}^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t} T_{e,t}^{\\text{in}}}{\\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t}} \\quad \\forall v, t\n  \\]\n  (Outgoing temperature at a node is the flow-weighted average of incoming temperatures.)\n- **Supply/return temperature bounds:**  \n  \\[\n  T_{i,t}^{\\min} \\leq T_{i,t} \\leq T_{i,t}^{\\max} \\quad \\forall i \\in \\text{suppliers}, t\n  \\]\n  (Ensures water temperatures remain within safe and efficient operating ranges.)\n- **Mass and energy conservation:**  \n  \\[\n  \\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t} = \\sum_{e \\in \\text{out}(v)} q_{e,t} \\quad \\forall v, t\n  \\]\n  (Total inflow equals total outflow at each node, ensuring no mass accumulation.)\n- **Thermal storage dynamics:**  \n  \\[\n  s_{j,t+1} = s_{j,t} + \\eta_{\\text{charge}} q_{j,t}^{\\text{charge}} - \\eta_{\\text{discharge}} q_{j,t}^{\\text{discharge}} \\quad \\forall j, t\n  \\]\n  (Storage state evolves based on charged/discharged energy, accounting for efficiency losses.)\n- **Cycle-breaking constraints:**  \n  For each fundamental cycle \\( C \\) in the network and each time \\( t \\), we enforce:\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{e \\in C} x_{e,t} \\leq |C| - 1\n  \\]\n  This ensures that not all edges in a cycle can have flow in the same direction, thereby preventing non-physical cyclic flows (i.e., flows that circulate endlessly in a loop without serving any demand). For example, in a 3-edge cycle, at most 2 edges can have flow in the same direction.\n- **Variable domains:**  \n  \\( q_{e,t} \\in \\mathbb{R} \\), \\( T_{v,t} \\in [T^{\\min}, T^{\\max}] \\), \\( s_{j,t} \\in [0, S_j^{\\max}] \\), \\( x_{e,t} \\in \\{0,1\\} \\)\n\nA full list of constraints and their mathematical forms is provided in the supplementary material.\n\n---\n\n**Algorithmic Enhancements:**  \n\n1. **Cycle-Breaking Constraints:**  \n   As described above, for each fundamental cycle \\( C \\), the constraint\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{e \\in C} x_{e,t} \\leq |C| - 1\n   \\]\n   is added at each time \\( t \\) to prevent non-physical cyclic flows. This is necessary because, in cyclic networks, it is otherwise possible for flows to circulate endlessly without serving any demand, which is not physically meaningful.\n\n2. **Supply/Return Symmetry Reduction:**  \n   Many networks have symmetric supply and return paths. To reduce redundant search over equivalent solutions, we identify automorphisms in the network graph and add symmetry-breaking constraints, such as:\n   \\[\n   q_{i_1,t} \\geq q_{i_2,t} \\geq \\ldots \\geq q_{i_k,t}\n   \\]\n   for suppliers \\( i_1, i_2, \\ldots, i_k \\), enforcing a lexicographic order on supplier flows.\n\n3. **Aggregated Temperature Mixing Constraints:**  \n   For nodes where multiple consumers are connected at a single junction, we aggregate their mixing constraints:\n   \\[\n   T_{\\text{agg},t}^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{i} q_{i,t} T_{i,t}}{\\sum_{i} q_{i,t}}\n   \\]\n   where \\( i \\) indexes the aggregated consumers. For example, if three consumers at a node each receive 2, 3, and 5 units of flow at 60\u00b0C, 65\u00b0C, and 70\u00b0C, the outgoing temperature is \\( (2 \\times 60 + 3 \\times 65 + 5 \\times 70)/10 = 66.5^\\circ \\)C.\n\n4. **Primal Heuristics:**  \n   We implement a greedy heuristic to quickly generate feasible solutions. For each consumer node, the heuristic assigns flow from the nearest available supplier (in terms of network distance), subject to pressure and temperature bounds. The procedure is as follows:\n   - Initialize all storage changes to zero.\n   - For each consumer, select the shortest path to a supplier with available capacity.\n   - Assign flow along this path to meet demand, updating supplier and pipe capacities.\n   - Repeat for all consumers.\n   This heuristic provides a feasible starting point for the global solver. Full pseudocode is provided in the supplementary material.\n\n5. **Tailored Branching Rules:**  \n   During branch-and-bound, we prioritize branching on variables with the highest impact on feasibility, such as:\n   - Flows on critical pipes (identified via betweenness centrality).\n   - Storage state variables at time steps with largest dual infeasibility.\n   For example, if the relaxed solution has a flow variable \\( q_{e,t} \\) with high fractional value and high dual infeasibility, it is branched first.\n\nFurther technical details and full algorithms for each enhancement are provided in the supplementary material."}}